<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Our Town Council – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Town Council</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Share Our Voice in the Community</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Take basic notes on a text about a familiar topic. Reading: Summarize arguments from general and academic articles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can take notes on town council meeting texts, recording key points on decisions and civic duties (e.g., "The city mayor stated that the Town Council is responsible for two main tasks: waste collection and road maintenance."). Can summarize arguments in articles about local governance, focusing on community involvement (e.g., "The article argues that citizens should attend town meetings to influence decisions."). Can outline civic responsibilities presented in articles, preparing for class discussions (e.g., "The importance of voting and community feedback is emphasized in the article.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting images of a town council meeting, a community clean-up, and citizens voting. Students are asked to briefly recall vocabulary from the previous lesson related to "community involvement" and "civic duties" in small groups. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What do these images show?" and "How do citizens participate in their community?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the skill of skimming for general understanding. The teacher quickly reviews a short, sample paragraph (unrelated to the main text) and demonstrates how to identify the main topic sentence and a few key words, ignoring details. The teacher also models the pronunciation of target vocabulary from Lesson 1, focusing on words with the short /ɪ/ sound (e.g., 'civic', 'public') and the /e/ sound followed by 'r' (e.g., 'share', 'serve').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main goal when you skim a text?" (Expected answer: To get the general idea or main topic.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you read every word when you skim?" (Expected answer: No, only key words and main sentences.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with a B1.2-level article titled "The Power of Your Voice: How Our Town Council Operates and Why Your Participation is Essential." The article (approx. 300-350 words) discusses the roles and responsibilities of the municipal government, public services provided, and the importance of citizen involvement through voting, feedback, and attending community meetings. It includes examples of passive voice ("Decisions are made based on community feedback."), reported speech ("The mayor said that new policies would be implemented soon."), and modals ("Citizens must vote to have a say in community decisions."). The register is formal and informative, typical of a local community newsletter or official website.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Take basic notes on a text about a familiar topic. Reading: Summarize arguments from general and academic articles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article once quickly (2-3 minutes) to get the main idea. They then choose the best title from a selection of three options provided by the teacher (e.g., a) "Our Town Council's History", b) "How Citizens Influence Local Decisions", c) "Problems with Public Services").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article a second time more carefully. They then evaluate five True/False statements related to the text's core information (e.g., "The Town Council is mainly responsible for national security." or "Community feedback is essential for making local decisions.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on identifying and understanding key information, arguments, and civic responsibilities presented in the text. Students practice extracting specific details and understanding the use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals in the context of local governance. Target vocabulary includes: <i>town council, community, voice, feedback, decisions, public services, municipal government, authorities, responsibilities, involvement, citizens, participation, voting, civic duties, operate, provide, participate, vote, report, attend, prioritize, contribute, responsible, essential, community-based, informed, participatory.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Note-Taking for Key Points (Controlled Practice):</b> Students reread the article with a specific task: to identify and list 3-4 main civic responsibilities or ways citizens can participate, as mentioned in the text. They also underline all instances of passive voice and reported speech. Example: "The article states that citizens <u>must vote</u> in local elections."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Summarizing Arguments (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs, students select one paragraph from the article and write a one-sentence summary of its main argument or point. They are encouraged to use at least one modal verb (can, should, must) and one piece of target vocabulary. The teacher circulates, offering support and ensuring accuracy in summarization and language use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Discussion & Vocabulary Check (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Pairs compare their identified civic responsibilities and paragraph summaries. They also check each other's underlined passive voice and reported speech examples. The teacher facilitates a brief whole-class discussion, asking, "What do you think are important duties for citizens mentioned in the article?" and "Citizens should vote to make their voices heard. Do you agree with this viewpoint expressed in the article?" (Pragmatic and Sociolinguistic elements).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Town Council".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Outline Creation & Mini-Report.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group is given a short, supplementary text (approx. 100-120 words) which is an excerpt from a hypothetical "Town Council Meeting Minutes" or a "Community Project Proposal" related to sustainability or public services. This text uses the same target vocabulary and grammatical structures. The task is to read this new text and create a structured outline that:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the main topic/issue discussed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Lists 2-3 key decisions or recommendations that were made (using passive voice if applicable, e.g., "A new waste collection program was approved.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes any reported statements or viewpoints from officials or citizens (e.g., "The councilor stated that community feedback would be crucial.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Outlines any immediate civic responsibilities or next steps for citizens (e.g., "Citizens should attend the next meeting.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After creating the outline, each group prepares a brief (1-2 minute) oral "mini-report" to the class, summarizing their outline's key points. The focus is on clear, concise communication of information extracted through reading, demonstrating understanding of the text's arguments and details.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on group collaboration and the quality of the written outline and oral mini-report.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' written outlines and their group's oral mini-report based on the supplementary text from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy of Notes/Summary:</b> Are the main ideas, decisions, and civic responsibilities accurately identified and clearly outlined/summarized from the text?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>decisions, responsibilities, involvement, participation, provide, contribute</i>) from the lesson accurately used in the outline and report?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Application of Grammar:</b> Is there evidence of correct use of passive voice, reported speech, and modals (e.g., "The plan was approved," "The committee recommended that...", "Citizens must engage") when referring to the text's content?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute to the reading, outlining, and reporting process?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Is the pronunciation of target words and phrases generally clear and understandable, particularly those with /ɪ/ and /e/ sounds, and correct word stress in multisyllabic words?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new things you learned about civic duties from today's reading."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in taking notes from an article about community issues (1=not confident, 5=very confident)?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the reading activities today helped you the most in understanding how to summarize information?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the information you read today help you share your voice in your own community?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in extracting and summarizing key information. The combination of gist reading, note-taking, and outlining helped reinforce comprehension skills. The use of supplementary texts ensured the reading focus was maintained throughout. Some students struggled with distinguishing between main arguments and supporting details when summarizing; scaffolding with sentence starters for summaries might be beneficial in future lessons. The integration of pronunciation practice for /ɪ/ and /e/ sounds also requires continued attention and perhaps more focused drills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The outlines and summaries created today will serve as a foundation for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction). Students will use the information and arguments from these texts to express their opinions, debate, and engage in structured discussions about community involvement and civic responsibilities, further practicing target vocabulary and grammar in an interactive context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>